At about $41.66 and a ~$7.3B market cap, it is still small enough that a successful commercial transition could re-rate it dramatically. The upside thesis isn’t just “AI likes nuclear”: Oklo’s Groves test reactor achieved first criticality this month, which is a genuine technical milestone. But the commercial business remains early, and the sector has been punished precisely because licensing, capital requirements, and deployment timelines can stretch into the 2030s. ⁠￼

How I’d actually play it

I’d create a dedicated Goblin Grenade position:

\boxed{\text{OKLO = 1\%-2\% of total portfolio max initially}}

Not because I only expect 1–2% upside—the opposite. The small allocation is what allows us to survive being very wrong while retaining meaningful exposure to a 3×–5× outcome.

My staged entry:

BUY 0.75% now around the low-$40s.

ADD 0.5% if it falls roughly into the $35–38 region without the fundamental thesis deteriorating.

ADD another 0.5–0.75% only on hard confirmation, such as meaningful commercial licensing/deployment progress or a materially stronger contracted-customer/financing milestone.

That distinction matters:

\text{price down}
\neq
\text{automatically buy more}

We add because either price improves while thesis stays intact, or thesis improves while price hasn’t fully reacted.

Why OKLO rather than RKLB as the grenade?

Rocket Lab is the better company today by conventional operating evidence: Q2 revenue was a record $234M, backlog reached $2.36B, and it has more than 90 launches in backlog. ⁠￼

But RKLB is already around a $46B market cap. That’s still exciting, but a 5× requires something like a $230B valuation.

OKLO at ~$7.3B can 5× into ~$37B.

That is much easier mathematically if the technology genuinely crosses from speculative project to commercial infrastructure.

So:

\text{RKLB}=\text{higher-confidence moonshot}

\boxed{\text{OKLO}=\text{higher-convexity moonshot}}

What could make OKLO explode upward

The pattern we’re hunting is our Probability Collapse pattern.

Today the market has to price:

Will this technology actually get licensed, financed, built, and economically operated?

Every major milestone can remove one branch of uncertainty:

\text{technical proof}
\rightarrow
\text{regulatory proof}
\rightarrow
\text{customer proof}
\rightarrow
\text{economic proof}

As uncertainty collapses:

P(\text{commercial success})
\uparrow

and the valuation can move discontinuously rather than gradually.

That’s exactly the pattern that made the hindsight ABCL example interesting.

What kills the trade

The Goblin exits or substantially cuts if we see:

* material licensing setbacks;
* repeated deployment delays;
* customer commitments failing to convert;
* capital requirements ballooning;
* severe dilution becoming necessary;
* evidence that electricity economics don’t work;
* competing reactor approaches commercializing substantially faster.

And I’d accept that this could fall 50%+. That’s why it belongs in the grenade compartment rather than the core portfolio.

